From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SplitPlay <splitplay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 12:09:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] splitplay: --self-test exercises capture, input and audio in
 the session

Without a Moonlight client there was no way to show that a worker started
in a desk session actually captures that session. The new option
--self-test <seconds> (1-600, standalone only: not with --pipe) runs
after the usual start-up (platform, input and encoder probing, which
already opens the session's display) and then, for <seconds>:

- captures the session's display through the capture path of the chosen
  encoder (DXGI Desktop Duplication into GPU memory for hardware
  encoders, system memory for the software encoder), with the cursor
  blended in from the DDA pointer shape and the same reinit handling as a
  stream (desktop switches, mode changes);
- moves the mouse back and forth with SendInput (relative moves) and
  checks with GetCursorPos that the session's cursor follows;
- opens the session's default render endpoint through WASAPI loopback
  and reads 100 packets of 5 ms.

The result is logged, printed and written to <desk-dir>/self-test.json:

  {"version":"0.1.0","session":2,"encoder":"software","capture_memory":"system",
   "capture":{"frames":42,"timeouts":17,"reinits":0,"width":1280,"height":720},
   "input":{"moves":30,"cursor_moved":true},
   "audio":{"ok":true,"endpoint":"{0.0.0.00000000}.{...}","packets":100},"ok":true}

The exit code is 0 when at least one frame was captured, 1 otherwise. CI
uses it to check a worker started as SYSTEM in an RDP session; on a host
it is a quick diagnosis of a desk.

video::chosen_encoder_info() exposes the probed encoder's name and capture
memory type for this.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
